    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "There are online Black video gaming communities, such as the Black Simmer Facebook Group and Tumblr blog, confronting video game customization limitations today by developing a distinct style of play: Black video game modifications, or modding. This Black stylization practice is undertaken by Black gamers to modify video game content—such as an avatar's appearance—to integrate into their gaming experiences more culturally resonant details, symbols, or artifacts from Black Diasporic expressive cultures and vernacular imaginaries often overlooked by mainstream digital media. In turn, these players create their own Black playgrounds that collectively celebrate, affirm, and attempt to sustain Blackness in its totality by caring for its expansive manifestation in the digital worlds their video games allow them to play in. Black modding enables Black gamers to challenge archetypal or stereotypical images of, and narratives about, their racial identity in video gaming landscapes and discourses online. This research study explores how Black modding is also a praxis of technological appropriation for reclamation, enabling Black gamers to feel good about—or derive pleasure from—gaming with more expansive and holistic Black representations than what their video games afford them by default. Black modding is Black pleasure activism: a way for Black gamers to reclaim their whole, happy, and satisfiable Black selves from the limitations technological oppression imposes upon Black expression. The existence of Black modding and the circulation of Black world-making capabilities as creative content, such as TikToks, YouTube videos, or #InternetChallenges, reflects Black communal prioritization of pleasure about their Blackness. I utilize three qualitative research methodologies and artistic methods to observe the Black being-to-digital world connection as a dialectic of Black players confronting and choosing to counter their racial oppression when playing their video games, especially life simulators like The Sims, by prioritizing pleasure in their moments of desired reprieve in Black digital worlds. \r\n\r\nMy chosen artistic methods are paired with qualitative research methodologies to provide a nuanced exploration of digital Blackness centered on Black play as an Afro-presentist and activist-tool. The stylization of the literature review into a magazine makes Black modding as a phenomenon not only visually palpable through content creation, but academically resonant with Black studies discourse about technological innovation. This allows readers to see how Black modding continues a legacy of Black technological appropriation needed to manifest more expansive, and thus more holistic, Black imagery and stories in video games today. Throughout this magazine, post-phenomenology is employed as a qualitative research methodology to spotlight the historical American precedent of Black technological oppression. The accompanying podcast utilizes narrative inquiry to explore how Black modding grants Black gamers agency over their relationship with digital Blackness, granting them freedom to engage with Blackness in ways that make them happy. Lastly, a digital archive housing the magazine and podcast episode models how Black gamers use Black modding to satisfy their desires for Black digital places as playgrounds. I embraced an autoethnographic approach towards designing this archive to gain firsthand insight into the extensive effort and time required by Black digital users, such as gamers, to reshape digital environments. Together, these artistic methods and research methodologies scaffold an ancestory for Black liberation movements in the Digital Age. Black modding as an act of affirming Black aliveness in the Digital Age is personally decided, communally negotiated, and should be recognized as an orgasmic yes to humanness. Black modding not only confronts the existential threat posed by the digital restriction of Black  expression, but spotlights how Black gamers actively rely on digital play technology today to prioritize Black pleasure in the present moment.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
